ZERO MEDIA 0.14 - VERIFICATION RECORD
2026-11-20

Implementation: original Zero only. Validation infrastructure and platform
firmware are external to the delivered implementation. No physical disk
was written by these checks. User data files remained unchanged.

FINAL SYSTEM IMAGE
Container: system.zmedia, 18878464 bytes.
Container SHA256:
cff6bdf74fc41545c6cbc3087bbb02d8defcdc61bda0bb436b18c5d03adf3325
Disk payload:18874368 bytes, 512-byte logical sectors.
Payload SHA256:
006703b31f8d9a3a3302066da43fce2c07551eba4f87f4bb935165453d75eec6
The custom ZMEDIA01 header is separate from the MBR/ESP disk payload.
The image was generated from our compiled artifacts without reading an ISO.

OWN COMPILER AND COMPLETE BUILD
The updated original compiler rebuilds to identical bytes.
182 existing compiler checks pass, plus32 call7 target/argument checks.
The new x64 raw/EFI call7 ABI passed128 rounds with seven arguments,
nested calls, stack alignment, shadow space writes and arena preservation.
A BIOS64 coroutine/arena test passed. Actual UEFI menu launches cover
LoadImage/StartImage. IA32 call7 code and target behavior are unchanged.
tools/build.zero produces66artifacts in70stages. All fixed-slot limits pass.
Compiler IA32 app is141673 bytes, below159744. The final full build is
outside the product source at validation-0.14/build-release.

DISK BUILDER AND REAL EMULATED USB BOOT
97 checks passed for the final payload:58 actual USB boot checks across
BIOS64, BIOS32, UEFI64 and UEFI32;6 menu/writer-return checks;29 structural
and malformed-input checks;4 boundary and readback-race regressions.
Each boot uses QEMU USB mass storage, not an attached CD image. The system
reaches its terminal, runs both built-in language compilers and verifies
private ATA data persistence across a cold restart. Both UEFI menus launch
the real firmware writer and can return and start the OS afterward.
Linux and Windows/Wine zero-disk builders produce identical disk bytes.
See disk-verification.ztext for the independent detail.

FORMAT AND SHA256
The original Zero format self-check passes8712 cases on Linux and again
on Windows/Wine: known hashes, every header-byte mutation, every truncated
length of a minimal image, appended data, changed payload and invalid mode.
Independent external checks:166 Linux codec cases,162 Windows/Wine cases,
and145 SHA256 vectors including block boundaries and the million-a vector.
See FORMAT-VERIFICATION.ztext and REVIEW.ztext.

NATIVE FLASH WRITER
Independent CLI checks:63 Linux and61 Windows/Wine, covering full source
validation, regular-file output, nonreplacement, corrupt images and
rejection of invalid targets before any destructive operation.
The exact raw-I/O backend passes18 transfer/failure cases plus cleanup on
each host using private regular files. Guard bytes remain unchanged.
The production coordinator passes32 additional cases on each host with
private-file device discovery: confirmations, capacity boundaries, all
short backup-GPT tails, identity changes, flush failures, corrupt readback
and read-only comparisons. Mock discovery does not certify USB discovery.
The final18MiB image was written, flushed and read back by both final native
executables; both results match the final disk payload byte for byte.
Actual removable-device hardware on Linux or Windows remains untested.

FIRMWARE FLASH WRITER
25 actual firmware scenarios pass across the two UEFI editions. The final
integrated flow passes on both: boot the exact published USB payload, open
Flash Writer from its menu, select the final image on another source volume,
write a third USB device, verify it, return to the menu and start Zero OS.
Both final UEFI editions perform real emulated USB writes, flushes and
full readback. The source and boot disks are excluded from selection.
Bounds and failures include cancellation, read-only/nonremovable targets,
bad image hashes, USB removal, exact-size and one-extra-sector disks.
An IA32 sparse USB disk larger than2TiB verifies both LBA words and correct
backup-GPT tail location while preserving the low-address alias guard.
IA32 allocations are constrained below4GiB. Device discovery connects
firmware-supported controllers before scanning other source/target ports.
The final detailed VM matrix is in firmware-verification.ztext.
Firmware binaries from the complete build match the tested binaries:
